Creating a full-fledged Chrome plugin involves several steps, including writing the code, packaging it, and publishing it on the Chrome Web Store. However, I can give you a simple example of how you can create a Chrome extension that saves the contents of the current page.

First, create a directory for your extension and create a `manifest.json` file inside it. This file tells Chrome about your extension's permissions and functionality. Here's an example `manifest.json` file:

```json
{
  "manifest_version": 2,
  "name": "Page Saver",
  "version": "1.0",
  "description": "Saves the contents of the current page",
  "permissions": [
    "activeTab",
    "downloads"
  ],
  "browser_action": {
    "default_icon": "icon.png",
    "default_title": "Save Page"
  },
  "background": {
    "scripts": ["background.js"],
    "persistent": false
  }
}
```

Next, create an `icon.png` file in the same directory. This file will be the icon for your extension. It should be a 128x128 pixel PNG image.

Now, create a `background.js` file. This file will run in the background when your extension is loaded. It will listen for clicks on the browser action and save the page when it's clicked. Here's an example `background.js` file:

```javascript
chrome.browserAction.onClicked.addListener(function(tab) {
  chrome.tabs.executeScript({
    code: 'document.body.innerHTML'
  }, function(html) {
    var blob = new Blob([html[0]], {type: "text/html;charset=utf-8"});
    chrome.downloads.download({
      url: URL.createObjectURL(blob),
      filename: 'page.html',
      saveAs: true
    });
  });
});
```

This code saves the current page's HTML as a file called `page.html`. Note that this only saves the HTML, not any resources like images or stylesheets.

To load your extension, open Chrome and go to `chrome://extensions`. Enable "Developer mode" by clicking the toggle in the top right corner. Then, click "Load unpacked extension..." and select the directory containing your extension.

When you click the extension's icon, it will save the current page's HTML as a file.

This is a very basic example, and there are many ways you could improve it. For example, you could save the page as a zip file containing all the resources, or you could prompt the user for a filename instead of always using `page.html`.

