To extract the text from an HTML document using JavaScript, you can create a function that uses the `textContent` property. This property returns the textual content of a node and its descendants. However, be aware that this might include text from all elements in the document, not just the visible text. Here's a function to help you achieve that:

```javascript
function extractTextFromDocument() {
  // Get the document's body
  const body = document.body;

  // Remove any non-textual nodes (like script or style) to clean up the output
  const nonTextualNodes = body.querySelectorAll('script, style, noscript, iframe');
  nonTextualNodes.forEach(node => node.remove());

  // Get the textual content of the document's body
  const text = body.textContent;

  // Remove any excessive white spaces
  const cleanText = text.replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim();

  return cleanText;
}
```

This function first gets the document's body, then removes any non-textual nodes such as `<script>`, `<style>`, `<noscript>`, and `<iframe>` elements to clean up the output. After that, it gets the textual content of the document's body, and finally, it removes any excessive white spaces and returns the clean text.

To use this function, simply call it:

```javascript
const text = extractTextFromDocument();
console.log(text);
```

Keep in mind that this function will return the text from the entire document. If you want to extract text from a specific element, you can modify the function to accept a parameter that specifies the element you want to extract the text from.

