Certainly! Below is a simplified version of a Kalman filter implemented in GNU Octave. This example assumes a linear system with both the process and measurement equations being linear. The Kalman filter equations used here are the standard discrete-time Kalman filter equations.

```octave
% Define the system matrices
A = [1, 1; 0, 1];  % State transition matrix
H = [1, 0];        % Measurement matrix
Q = eye(2);        % Process noise covariance
R = 1;             % Measurement noise covariance
P = eye(2);        % Estimate uncertainty covariance

% Initial state estimate
x_hat = [0; 0];

% Kalman filter function
function x_est = kalman_filter(z, x_est, P, A, H, Q, R)
    % Predict
    x_pred = A * x_est;
    P_pred = A * P * A' + Q;

    % Update
    K = P_pred * H' / (H * P_pred * H' + R);
    x_est = x_pred + K * (z - H * x_pred);
    P = (eye(size(P)) - K * H) * P_pred;
end

% Generate some synthetic data for testing
N = 100;
z = zeros(N, 1); % Measurement data
for i = 1:N
    z(i) = x_hat(1) + 0.1 * randn(); % Add some measurement noise
    x_hat = [x_hat(1) + x_hat(2); 0.1 * randn()]; % Update true state
end

% Apply the Kalman filter
x_estimates = zeros(N, 2);
for i = 1:N
    x_estimates(i, :) = kalman_filter(z(i), x_est, P, A, H, Q, R);
end

% Plot the results
figure();
plot(1:N, z, 'b', 1:N, x_estimates(:, 1), 'r');
xlabel('Time');
ylabel('Value');
legend('Measurements', 'Kalman Filter Estimate');
```

This code assumes a simple one-dimensional position and velocity system with state vector `[position; velocity]`. The process noise covariance matrix `Q` and measurement noise `R` are set to identity and 1 respectively for simplicity. You can adjust these values to better fit the characteristics of your system.

The `kalman_filter` function takes in the current measurement, the current state estimate and covariance matrix, the system matrices `A`, `H`, `Q`, and `R`, and updates the state estimate and covariance matrix based on the Kalman filter equations.

Note that this is a very basic implementation and might need to be adapted to your specific use case. For example, if your system is nonlinear, you would need to use an Extended Kalman Filter or another appropriate nonlinear filtering technique.

