```

4. Then, you can check if the user's IP address is in the array of allowed IP addresses:

```php
if (!in_array($userIp, $allowedIps)) {
    abort(403, 'Your IP address is not allowed to access this resource.');
}
```

5. Finally, if the user's IP address is in the array of allowed IP addresses, you can call the `$next` closure to allow the request to proceed:

```php
return $next($request);
```

Putting it all together, your `CheckIp` middleware might look something like this:

```php
namespace App\Http\Middleware;

use Closure;
use Illuminate\Http\Request;

class CheckIp
{
    public function handle(Request $request, Closure $next)
    {
        $allowedIps = ['192.168.1.1', '192.168.1.2', '192.168.1.3'];
        $userIp = $request->ip();

        if (!in_array($userIp, $allowedIps)) {
            abort(403, 'Your IP address is not allowed to access this resource.');
        }

        return $next($request);
    }
}
```

Remember to register the middleware in the `app/Http/Kernel.php` file before using it.

