In PHP 8.2, you can use the ` concurrent` package from the `amphp` library to manage a pool of coroutines. Here's an example of how you can use it to limit the number of running coroutines to 5:
```php
require_once 'vendor/autoload.php';

use Amp\Concurrent\Pool;

$pool = new Pool(5); // create a pool of 5 workers

$jobs = [
    // add your jobs here
];

$promises = [];

foreach ($jobs as $job) {
    $promises[] = $pool->submit($job); // submit the job to the pool
}

$promise = Promise\all($promises); // wait for all jobs to finish

$promise->wait(); // block until all jobs are completed
```

This will create a pool of 5 workers and submit each job to the pool. The pool will ensure that only 5 jobs are running at any time, and will queue the remaining jobs until a worker becomes available. The `Promise\all` function will wait for all jobs to finish before continuing.
Alternatively, you can use `Amp\Loop` to create a loop and run jobs one by one, up to a limit. Here's an example:
```less
require_once 'vendor/autoload.php';

use Amp\Loop;

$jobs = [
    // add your jobs here
];

$running = 0; // number of running coroutines

foreach ($jobs as $job) {
    Loop::defer(function () use ($job, &$running) {
        if ($running >= 5) { // if we have reached the limit, wait for a coroutine to finish
            Loop::delay(0, function () use (&$running) {
                $running--;
            });
        }

        $running++; // increment the number of running coroutines
        $job(); // run the job

        Loop::delay(0, function () use (&$running) {
            $running--; // decrement the number of running coroutines
        });
    });
}

Loop::run(); // run the loop
```

In this example, we use `Loop::defer` to schedule each job for execution. We keep track of the number of running coroutines and if we have reached the limit of 5, we use `Loop::delay` to wait for a coroutine to finish before starting a new one. After each job is finished, we decrement the number of running coroutines.
Note that in both examples, you need to replace the `$job` variable with the actual job that you want to run. A job can be a coroutine or a callable that returns a promise.

