Sure, I can create a simple banking app scenario using the Strategy pattern in JavaScript. The Strategy pattern enables you to define a family of algorithms, put each of them in separate classes, and make their objects interchangeable.

In this scenario, we're going to have a `BankingApp` class, which will use various strategies for different operations like depositing, withdrawing, and transferring money. Each strategy will be encapsulated in its own class.

```javascript
// Strategy pattern
class PaymentStrategy {
  execute(amount) {
    throw new Error('execute method must be implemented in subclass');
  }
}

class DepositStrategy extends PaymentStrategy {
  execute(amount) {
    console.log(`Depositing ${amount}...`);
  }
}

class WithdrawStrategy extends PaymentStrategy {
  execute(amount) {
    console.log(`Withdrawing ${amount}...`);
  }
}

class TransferStrategy extends PaymentStrategy {
  execute(amount, target) {
    console.log(`Transferring ${amount} to ${target}...`);
  }
}

// Context class
class BankingApp {
  constructor(strategy) {
    this.strategy = strategy;
  }

  setStrategy(strategy) {
    this.strategy = strategy;
  }

  executeStrategy(amount, target = null) {
    if (target) {
      this.strategy.execute(amount, target);
    } else {
      this.strategy.execute(amount);
    }
  }
}

// Usage
const bankingApp = new BankingApp(new DepositStrategy());
bankingApp.executeStrategy(100);

bankingApp.setStrategy(new WithdrawStrategy());
bankingApp.executeStrategy(50);

bankingApp.setStrategy(new TransferStrategy());
bankingApp.executeStrategy(25, 'Friend');
```

In this example:

1. We define a base `PaymentStrategy` class with an abstract `execute` method.
2. We create three strategy classes (`DepositStrategy`, `WithdrawStrategy`, and `TransferStrategy`) that extend `PaymentStrategy` and implement the `execute` method.
3. We create a `BankingApp` class that takes a strategy as a constructor argument and provides a method to set a new strategy and execute the current strategy.
4. We use the `BankingApp` class with different strategies and demonstrate how the behavior changes based on the strategy used.

This demonstrates how the Strategy pattern can make your code more flexible and easier to maintain when dealing with multiple, interchangeable behaviors in a banking app scenario.

