To create a Rust program that reads URLs from a file, separates YouTube URLs from other URLs, and downloads the YouTube URLs using `yt-dlp`, you need to have Rust installed on your system. Additionally, you need to have `yt-dlp` installed in your system's PATH. Below is a Rust program that accomplishes the task:

```rust
use std::fs::File;
use std::io::{BufRead, BufReader};
use std::process::Command;

fn main() {
    let file = File::open("urls.txt").expect("Failed to open file");
    let reader = BufReader::new(file);

    for line in reader.lines() {
        let url = line.unwrap();
        if is_youtube_url(&url) {
            download_youtube_video(&url);
        }
    }
}

fn is_youtube_url(url: &str) -> bool {
    url.starts_with("https://www.youtube.com")
        || url.starts_with("https://youtube.com")
}

fn download_youtube_video(url: &str) {
    let output = Command::new("yt-dlp")
        .arg(url)
        .output()
        .expect("Failed to execute yt-dlp command");

    if output.status.success() {
        println!("Downloaded video from: {}", url);
    } else {
        eprintln!("Failed to download video from: {}", url);
    }
}
```

This program assumes there is a file named `urls.txt` in the same directory as the program, containing one URL per line. The `is_youtube_url` function checks if a URL is a YouTube URL by checking if it starts with the expected YouTube URL patterns. The `download_youtube_video` function uses `yt-dlp` to download the video.

